/spawn -o <file> no longer replaces a file that's already there unless you add --force. The check runs before the sub-agent starts, so a refused run costs nothing. It's committed (656a56f), and fmt, clippy with -D warnings and the full cargo test all pass.

I titled it "Part of #1211", not "Fixes". The ticket covers every command that writes to a file the user names, and two still overwrite silently:
• --save-session <path> (task 1 found it).
• The CLI --output/-o <f> flag in src/prompt_utils.rs, which this task's search found.

Both are flags used in scripts, where re-running with the same file name on purpose is normal (shell >, curl -o and gcc -o all replace). So they need one decision together, and I put that question on #1211.

What changed, checked with the built binary in a temp repo (junk key, dead local address, `a.md` holding `hi`):
• /spawn -o a.md say hi → a.md already exists. Nothing was run. To replace it: /spawn --force -o a.md say hi (or choose another file name). The file still holds hi and nothing was started.
• /spawn -o . say hi → refused as a directory, with no --force offered.
• /spawn --force say hi → --force only applies to -o <file>. Nothing was run.
• When the file appears mid-run: without --force the save uses create-only mode, so a file created while the sub-agent ran is kept.
• Behaviour change beyond the ticket: when a save fails for any reason, the answer is now kept rather than lost. It still goes into the conversation, or stays collectable with /spawn collect for --bg. The task shows as failed with "output NOT saved: …". Before, a failed write threw the answer away.

The tests are in a new file, src/commands_spawn_output_tests.rs. They cover:
• where each word of the command ends up;
• feeding the printed fix back through the parser to get the same file, task and flags;
• a refused run registering nothing;
• an existing file kept byte-for-byte, and --force replacing it.

For the recall control, I broke the start check and the create-only write one at a time. The matching test failed by name each time, and both passed again once restored. Before-and-after results and the control are posted on #1211.

Search for other file writes: 39 places in the code write files, and 7 of them write to a file the user names. Five refuse to overwrite (/export, /save, /spawn -o, /init, yoyo skill init) and two overwrite silently (the two flags above). The other 32 are supposed to change the file (/config set, the setup wizard, which backs up first, rename/move/refactor, checkpoint restores) or write yoyo's own files.

Separate bug found: -o "a b.md" splits the file name at the space, so the file is named "a and the rest becomes part of the task. I filed it as #1213; the docs say -o takes a one-word file name.

Also touched: [--force] added to the /spawn help, the usage text and docs/src/usage/commands.md, and the commands_spawn.rs line limit raised from 4570 to 4629 in tests/module_size.rs. One test calls the real /spawn handler. If the refusal ever broke, that test would start a real sub-agent run and create a git worktree in the repo during cargo test.
